From b466cfa846be5692af82b18a7c2c32937ddaa1fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 21:10:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: mask literal unquoted heredoc data
 in the executed-path walk

`bash -l close.sh` refused with "kanban.db is a SQLite database open in this
gateway process" when the script held an UNQUOTED heredoc whose body had call
syntax on the db path (`python3 - <<EOF\nsqlite3.connect("DB")\nEOF`,
`cat <<EOF\nf(DB)\nEOF`). In a startup-file shell's walk alias names are
unknown (76e3ffa400), the unquoted body stayed visible, and `NAME ( x )` read
as an alias-led subshell executing the db.

Rule: in the executed-path walk only (nested=True), an unquoted heredoc body
with no `$`, backtick or backslash is masked exactly like its quoted twin,
behind the same consumer/shadow/output-sink fences. Such a body is
byte-identical to the `<<'EOF'` form, which 76e3ffa400 already masks under
unknown aliases, so this reopens nothing that rule closed. Expansion- or
escape-bearing bodies stay visible (backslash-newline can even move the
delimiter). Text scans are unchanged: `cat <<EOF\nhermes gateway restart\nEOF`
under `bash -l` still refuses, and a script path named in a masked body is
still read.

Tests (tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_login_heredoc_data.py):
- both data shapes allowed under bash -l / bash / sh / source, quoted and not;
- still refused under bash -l: `alias cat=bash` (with and without
  `shopt -s expand_aliases`) + unquoted body holding `hermes gateway restart`
  or `bash danger.sh`; $(...)/backtick bodies; `| bash`; body written to a
  later-run file; 80 real-bash oracle cases.
- test_lifecycle_guard_heredoc_walk: the "expansion-capable body" test now
  carries a real expansion (`$HOME`); its literal twin is pinned equal to the
  quoted form.

RED on main 5d9e537979b: 4 failures (both bash -l data shapes, the literal
mask boundary, the quoted-twin equality). GREEN: tests/cron 282 files /
11992 passed; tests/tools self_repo + heredoc_background guards 168 passed.

The historical close_1235.sh on laptop-1 (read via hub) uses QUOTED
heredocs; its refusal is a separate shape, not claimed fixed here.
Card: t_8e9378b380c66089.
